import Foundation

/// #853: three small MPEG-2 clips for the inverse telecine tests, made offline with the FFmpeg CLI (9.0.2)
/// and embedded so no GPL filter (`telecine`, `tinterlace`) is needed at build or test time. All are
/// 96x64, 29.97 fps (30000/1001), Matroska, the picture a smooth pattern moving sideways (`geq`), so the
/// fields of a film frame match and fields from different instants comb. The two interlaced clips decode
/// like an NTSC DVD: every frame `interlaced_frame=1`, `top_field_first=1`, `repeat_pict=0`.
///
/// The pattern and the shared encoder settings:
///
///     PAT="geq=lum='128+90*sin(2*PI*(X+T*150)/40)*cos(2*PI*Y/80)':cb=128:cr=128"
///     ENC="-c:v mpeg2video -q:v 12 -g 30 -bf 2 -pix_fmt yuv420p -map_metadata -1 -fflags +bitexact"
///
/// Checked with `ffmpeg -i <clip> -vf "fieldmatch=order=auto:combmatch=none,idet" -f null -`: the
/// telecined clip reads 150 of 150 frames progressive, the interlaced one 150 of 150 combed (TFF).
